            ("Denotation vs Connotation", "Denotation is the strict literal dictionary definition of a word; Connotation comprises the cultural, emotional, and associative overtones it invokes", "Denotation is emotional feeling; Connotation is literal fact", "Both represent exact synonyms", "Denotation is rhyming; Connotation is rhythm"),
            ("Satire", "A literary genre or technique that uses irony, ridicule, sarcasm, and wit to expose, critique, and reform human vices, follies, and institutional corruptions", "A tragic eulogy for the dead", "A lighthearted love romance", "A strict historical biography"),
            ("Parody", "An artistic work that closely imitates the style, mannerisms, or language of a specific author, genre, or work for comic effect or critical ridicule", "A serious heroic epic", "A documentary narrative", "A philosophical treaty on rights"),
            ("Farce", "A low comedy characterized by absurd situations, exaggerated buffoonery, physical horseplay, mistaken identities, and rapid-fire ludicrous twists", "A dignified somber tragedy", "A religious liturgical drama", "An autobiographical confession"),
            ("Archetype", "A universally recognized symbol, character type, plot pattern, or motif that recurs across diverse world mythologies, cultures, and literature", "A unique idiosyncratic quirk", "A regional dialect term", "A modern technological innovation"),
            ("Hubris", "Overweening arrogance, excessive pride, or reckless insolence leading a tragic protagonist to disregard divine laws or moral limits, provoking downfall", "Gentle Christian humility", "Physical cowardice on battlefield", "Generous philanthropic charity"),
            ("Catharsis (Aristotle)", "The emotional purging, cleansing, and therapeutic release of pity (eleos) and fear (phobos) experienced by the audience at the close of a tragedy", "Intellectual confusion and anger", "Comic relief laughter", "Political rebellion against state"),
            ("Hamartia", "The tragic flaw, error of judgment, or constitutional blindness of a noble protagonist that sets their catastrophic downfall into motion", "A deliberate criminal conspiracy", "A comedic misunderstanding", "Divine intervention to save hero"),
            ("Foil Character", "A character whose personality, values, or behavior sharply contrasts with another character (usually the protagonist) to illuminate key traits", "The main narrator of events", "The author's personal mouthpiece", "A minor servant without lines"),
